# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Environment — never commit real credentials; .env.example is the tracked template
.env
.env.local
!.env.example

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# OS
.DS_Store

# Agent tooling state. Gate files, verifier evidence, session handoffs and research notes
# are the scaffolding used to build this, not the thing being delivered. The whole
# directory is excluded rather than named subdirectories, because the tooling adds new
# state paths over time and a per-path list silently stops covering them.
.claude/

# Internal working documents — kept locally, not shipped to the reviewer.
# The assignment brief, the build plan, the demo script and the slide skeleton are
# preparation material, not part of the delivered solution. The ADRs in docs/ADR/
# ARE part of the deliverable and stay tracked.
docs/problem.md
docs/repo.md
docs/video.md
docs/presentation.md
docs/deck.md

# The presentation frames rendered for the video and the deck. Same category as the script
# they serve: production assets, not the solution. Every figure on them is copied from
# DATA.md and ARCHITECTURE.md, which are tracked, so nothing unique is lost by excluding
# them. Delete this line to ship them.
docs/visuals/

# Personal interview preparation. These would be actively bad to ship: the Q&A scripts
# answers to questions the reviewer has not asked yet, and the study guide is a record of
# what its author was revising. Both are for the candidate, not the audience.
docs/drill_brief.md
docs/sql_study_guide.md
docs/interview_qa.md
docs/prep_card.md

# The same two categories again, under names the lists above did not anticipate. Both were
# sitting untracked and unignored, which is the state where one `git add -A` ships them.
#
# `important_things_to_add.md` is rehearsed spoken answers, including what to say when
# pushed back on, so it belongs with the Q&A scripts directly above. It also quotes session
# ids and tooling internals from the sessions that produced it, which are nobody's business
# outside this machine. `my_narration.md` is a spoken cut of the demo script, so it belongs
# with `docs/video.md` rather than here, and is listed in this block only to keep the two
# newly found files together.
docs/important_things_to_add.md
docs/my_narration.md

# Reference material from an unrelated project, used only as a formatting example.
# It describes a different client's system and must never ship in this deliverable.
sample/

# Runtime artifacts
*.log
!eval/results/*.log

# eval/results/ IS tracked on purpose: the README cites these numbers, and the raw
# per-question output is the evidence for them. A reviewer should be able to check the
# claim rather than take it on trust.
